so china's second largest port in the  eastern city of ningbo halted operations  on wednesday after an employee tested  positive for coronavirus the shutdown  could add more pressure to strained  global supply chains chinese ports are  still working to recover from our  typhoon in july and the shutdown of the  yantjian port in may after a coronavirus  infection the ningbo port is  particularly crucial to global trade  handling 19 million shipping containers  so far this year through july  and for more on that story let's bring  in fabian  journalist in beijing good to have you  with us fabian how serious are these new  kovit 19 outbreaks in china  that's a very interesting question  actually if you only look at the  absolute numbers then um the outbreaks  don't seem dangerous at all because  you have maybe 100 local infections on a  daily basis and 1.4 billion chinese  people living in the country but it's  the first time  since roughly a year that an infection  cluster has spread  from north to south basically  more than half of the provinces have  registered infections and just one case  is enough for the authorities to shut  down a whole port  because they have a really very strict  zero covet strategy and just in the last  days i visited one of the residential  compounds here in beijing that was also  sealed off only because of one case and  there are  roughly  24 um high-rise apartment buildings so  tens of thousands of people  cannot leave their buildings only  because of one infection so yeah it's  definitely very serious in that regard  right i mean you just mentioned beijing  zero kobe strategy while of course it is  commendable to try and curb the spread  of the virus as soon as possible it must  have an impact on the chinese economy  yeah that's a very a complex question  because last year the zero covert  strategy turned out to be very  successful also for the economy i mean  china was growing and because you had a  virus-free zone so to speak within the  country factories could work at normal  capacity you had a complete supply chain  in the country um however the pandemic  it's not a 100 meter sprint it's a  marathon and the strategy that was  successful last year um can be a burden  now because the economic costs are much  higher now you have the delta mutation  and the risk um despite a very strict  quarantine system of local inflict  sections and also  local infections that spread throughout  the whole country are much higher and  for every case you have to shut down a  factory you have to shut down a port  in may for example they had to shut down  the yentian port  near shenzhen and that was basically the  biggest incident  disrupting global supply chains that had  a bigger impact even than the incident  at the suez canal so at some point it  will become a problem however they are  trapped in this strategy not only  because they don't trust their  domestically produced vaccines also  because of the political propaganda last  year they already propagated the victory  against the virus and now that at least  that rhetoric leaves them not so much  space for an exit strategy right so the  virus is not playing ball there fabian  gretchen in beijing thank you so much  and of course we all know when china  sneezes the whole world is catching the  flu so conrad busan joining me in  frankfurt now uh what does all of this  mean for businesses and for consumers  here in europe  well monika i can tell you what it means  i went to an ikea store last week not on  business but you know to shop for stuff  and about one third of the products that  an ikea store in germany usually sells  is not available and the staff told me  that the things are either stuck on a  container ship somewhere between china  and germany or when somewhere in the  world a factory has a corona case it  often closes down immediately so it  can't deliver so we are really talking  about ripple effects in the global  supply chains here should germany try to  become more independent from all this a  majority of economists here in germany  are convinced that this is not a viable  way  you know not buying stuff from china  would make consumer prices much higher  here in germany and let's not forget  germany's economy is one of the most  interconnected worldwide  the exporters need the global supply and  delivery chains all right a lot at stake  there conrad business frankfurt thank  you so much
 

